        <body>
            <div xml:id="d168">

                <pb facs="0305" n="211" xml:id="pg211"/>

                <head>No. 168.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Denby</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-05-03"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 3,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 193.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received and read with much interest
                    your dispatch No. 328, of March 8 last, concerning the article by the Marquis
                    Tseng, entitled “China: the Sleep and the Awakening,” published in the Asiatic
                    Monthly Review.</p>
                <p>It is encouraging to see so astute an observer as the Marquis Tseng pay so high a
                    tribute to the friendly and just disposition of the United States toward China
                    and our firm respect for international obligations and comity.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
